WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.620
Welcome to fascinating frontiers, episode 103. It's June 16th, 2026.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:12.660
Some fascinating developments to cover today. China's Tianwen 2 Probe is executing a string of fine

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:18.880
burns to reach its target asteroid next month. China's Tianwen 2 spacecraft performed multiple

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:25.540
small propulsive maneuvers after its main burn on June 7. Radio tracking confirms the adjustments

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:31.380
are positioning the probe for an asteroid rendezvous in July. The sequence follows standard practice

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:37.600
for fine-tuning arrival trajectories at small solar system bodies. Engineers will monitor

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:43.100
the remaining corrections through the final approach phase. Next data releases are expected once

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:48.600
the spacecraft nears its target distance. If the sun were scaled to the size of a basketball and

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:54.300
Vancouver, the asteroid belt would lie roughly as far away as a single grain of sand on a beach

00:00:54.300 --> 00:01:01.100
in Halifax. Tianwen 2's recent burns illustrate how spacecraft reach such tiny targets across

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:07.160
hundreds of millions of kilometers. A main engine firing first drops the probe onto an intercept

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:13.660
trajectory, after which a series of much smaller thruster pulses each lasting only seconds correct

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:19.860
for tiny errors in position and velocity. These adjustments are measured by radio signals that travel

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:25.400
at the speed of light and return with timing precision measured in microseconds. The final

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:31.080
approach must occur within a window measured in tens of kilometers, otherwise the relative speed

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:37.420
will carry the spacecraft past its target forever. Scientists still cannot predict exactly how much

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:42.440
residual propellant will remain after the last correction, leaving open the question of whether

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:48.440
future missions can safely attempt a second rendezvous if the first attempt is missed. SpaceX

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:55.440
completed a funding round that brought total capital raised to $85.7 billion. The round included a

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:01.980
20% increase in share value and exercise of the green shoe option. Proceeds will support ongoing

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:07.780
vehicle development and constellation expansion. The valuation now places the company's market

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:14.680
capitalization above $2.5 trillion. Investors are watching how the capital deploys across

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:20.460
Starship and Starlink programs. From the precise navigation needed to reach small bodies,

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:25.160
we turn to what another spacecraft is revealing about the atmospheres of distant worlds.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:31.160
The James Webb Space Telescope captured detailed atmospheric data showing silicate clouds that

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:37.280
condense into ruby and sapphire rain. Observations reveal temperature contrasts and wind patterns

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:43.760
driving the unusual precipitation cycle. The planet's atmosphere contains high altitude clouds composed

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:51.040
of minerals that would be gems on Earth. With its unprecedented observational quality, JWST gives us

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:57.360
the most detailed glimpses into distant planets to date. Follow-up spectra are planned to track cloud

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:05.160
evolution over multiple orbits. The JWST observations of a distant exoplanet show silicate clouds condensing

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:12.400
into gemstone rain on the night side, while strong winds redistribute heat. The data separate the planet's

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:18.300
thermal emission from reflected starlight across a full orbit, revealing that earlier models had placed

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:24.320
the cloud deck too high. Refined temperature profiles now match the measured radius and suggest the

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:30.040
atmosphere is more transparent on the day side than expected. Follow-up programs will track whether

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:37.160
similar mineral clouds appear on other highly irradiated worlds. While JWST is refining our view of

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:43.040
one exoplanet's atmosphere, other researchers are correcting longstanding assumptions about another.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:49.360
Researchers used phase curve observations to separate day side and night side atmospheric signals

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:55.600
on a hot Jupiter. The asymmetric pattern showed that standard models had overestimated cloud coverage

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:01.540
on one hemisphere. Correcting for the asymmetry produced a revised temperature pressure profile

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:07.940
consistent with the planet's measured radius. The method relies on continuous monitoring across a

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:14.260
full orbit. Future observations will test whether the same correction applies to other inflated giants.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:21.080
From distant exoplanet atmospheres, we move closer to home, where a long ago human milestone is

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:28.300
being revisited. 63 years ago today, Valentina Tereshkova became the first woman to reach space aboard

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:35.420
Vostok 6. Her single orbit mission lasted just under three days and included manual orientation tests.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:40.740
The flight demonstrated that a non-pilot could operate a spacecraft under weightlessness.

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:45.680
Tereshkova's call sign, Seagull, remains part of Russian space lore.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:50.520
Our kivill footage and mission logs are being re-examined for training insights.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.520
Luca Parmentano described seeing Australian bushfires from the International Space Station during

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:02.800
a recent interview. The observations occurred while the fires burned across large areas of the continent.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:08.620
Parmentano noted the smoke plumes were visible even at night due to their thermal signature.

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:14.680
The account adds to the record of crew-earth observation contributions during long duration flights.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:21.540
Similar reports are expected from future Artemis crews. 60 years after that pioneering flight,

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:25.100
another cargo mission is wrapping up its own stay in orbit.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:32.160
The SpaceX Dragon spacecraft supporting CRS-34 will undock from the International Space Station today

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:37.940
after 30 days. Live coverage will track the departure and subsequent deorbit burn.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:42.820
The capsule carries experiment samples and station hardware for return to Earth.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:49.280
Splashdown is scheduled in the Pacific within 24 hours of undocking. Recovery teams are already

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:56.140
positioned for post-landing retrieval. A new analysis describes Saturn's moon Titan as holding vast

00:05:56.140 --> 00:06:02.100
stores of natural gas and liquid water beneath its surface. The reserves are likened to a Persian

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:09.080
gulf of space in scale. Data come from Cassini radar and infrared measurements combined with recent

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:15.360
climate models. The findings refine estimates of how much methane and ethane cycle through Titan's lakes

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:21.900
and atmosphere. Future missions could sample these reservoirs directly. While one spacecraft heads

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:28.640
home, another is preparing to head outward. Astrobotic unveiled its Griffin-1 lander ahead of environmental

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:35.080
testing in California. The mission, now called Moon Base 2 under NASA's Clips Program,

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:42.560
carries multiple payloads. Launch remains scheduled for late 2026. The lander will undergo vibration

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:48.140
and thermal vacuum trials before shipment to the launch site. Results from these tests will

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:54.380
determine final flight software uploads. NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory will open its campus to the

00:06:54.380 --> 00:07:01.880
public on October 10 and 11 to mark its 90th anniversary. Visitors can tour iconic facilities and explore

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:08.900
four thematic areas covering past missions and future plans. The event runs from 9.30 am to 4 pm

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:15.000
Pacific Daylight time each day. Registration details will be released closer to the date. Similar

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:21.860
open houses have drawn tens of thousands of attendees in prior years. An Earth-observing satellite captured

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:27.820
the Nebraska sand hills the largest dune system in the western hemisphere. The dunes cover roughly

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:34.120
one quarter of the state and remain active under prevailing winds. The image highlights seasonal vegetation

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:40.160
patterns across the rolling terrain. Scientists use such views to track changes in dune migration

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:46.400
and groundwater recharge. Comparable dune fields on other planets are studied with the same remote

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:52.660
sensing techniques. From upcoming lunar missions, we shift to a striking site visible in tonight's

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:59.540
evening sky. This evening, the moon passes three degrees north of Mercury, creating a visible triangle

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:06.200
with Jupiter low in the western sky. Observers can watch the alignment for roughly an hour after sunset.

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:12.340
Mercury reached greatest eastern elongation yesterday. It's farthest point from the sun in the

00:08:12.340 --> 00:08:18.340
evening sky. The geometry offers a brief window to spot the innermost planet without optical aid.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:25.140
No further alignments of this trio are expected until next month. Space exploration announced an

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:31.580
all-stock merger valued at $60 billion with cursor. The transaction would combine satellite and

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:37.940
software operations under a single listed entity. Regulatory filings are expected in the coming weeks.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:43.960
Shareholders will vote on the deal later this year. The merger follows the company's recent NASDAQ

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:50.540
listing. Space exploration technology's corporation has been included in the NASDAQ telecom index.

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:56.160
The addition reflects the company's growing role in satellite communications infrastructure.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:03.000
Index rebalancing takes effect at the next quarterly review. Fun managers tracking the index will

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:09.420
adjust holdings accordingly. The move follows the firm's recent public listing. Shares a space exploration

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:16.000
technology's corporation climbed 20% overnight following confirmation of increased IPO proceeds.

